  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Below normal low temperatures give way to a \nwarming trend early this week. \n\nHigh pressure will drift southwest through Monday. As a result,\nexpect a continuation of frost and freeze conditions tonight \nwith seasonable temperatures during the daylight hours.\n\nAfter the chilly start, temperatures will rebound well into the\n60s for much of the area this afternoon. Some stratocumulus are\npossible at times, but may ultimately be thin and transient.\n\nAs the high drifts southwest tonight, low-level flow will turn \nwesterly allowing for less of a marine influence. This should \nset us up for one more good radiational cooling night before \ntemperatures warm under return flow early in the workweek. \nTonight will be more of a ridge- valley split type of night with\ncooler conditions in the valleys/cold pockets/locations along \nand east of I-95 compared to the ridge tops given the sharp \nsurface inversion/decoupling.\n\nOnly minor changes have been made to the previous min temperature \nforecasts, which are near or below the coldest guidance which \nperforms best in radiational cooling (MOS, NBM 10th percentile and \nCanadian Regional).\n\nExpect temperatures to rise above normal (upper 60s to lower 70s) by \nMonday afternoon as the warming airmass mixes to the surface. \nMinimal cloud cover and light winds will remain persistent.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Above normal temperatures and dry conditions \ncontinue through the end of the week.\n\nAn upper-level ridge will build through the middle of the week \nsupporting above normal temperatures. A dry cold front will \ncross the area Tuesday, only dropping temperatures slightly. \nHigh pressure settles south of the area during the second half \nof the week supporting a warming trend again. A weak upper-level\ndisturbance will traverse the region Thursday night only \nbringing high clouds ahead of it. It looks like no precipitation \nwill occur through the remainder of October.",
